UP-3 — `replace_event_but_keep_existing_sub_events` silently deleted the sub-events
=================================================================================

SEVERITY: medium (silent data loss).  FILE: 1 (+ spec).

THE ISSUE
---------
`applyEventsChanges` supports a replacement relation called
`replace_event_but_keep_existing_sub_events`, whose whole contract is that the
replaced event's children survive and are re-attached to the first replacement
event. The code did this:

```js
// BEFORE — newIDE/app/src/EditorFunctions/ApplyEventsChanges.js
if (newEvents.getEvent(0).canHaveSubEvents()) {
  existingSubEvents.moveTo(newEvents.getEvent(0));
} else {
  // <-- no else branch at all
}
```

When the first replacement event cannot host sub-events — a comment event, or
a condition — the sub-events were simply dropped. The caller received
`success: true`. The condition below then destroyed the originals:

```js
existingEvent.getEvents().clear();   // the sub-events are gone, silently
```

The user loses events with no error and no undo trail. This is the worst
shape a correctness bug can have: destructive, silent, and reachable from an
ordinary authoring request ("replace this event but keep what it contained"
against a list whose replacement starts with a comment).

THE FIX
-------
Report the loss instead of hiding it. The cleanup still runs — the relation's
primary effect is the replacement, and failing that would leave the project in
a half-applied state, which is worse — but the caller is now told:

```diff
               existingSubEvents.moveTo(newEvents.getEvent(0));
+            } else {
+              // The sub-events cannot be moved to the first new event: report
+              // the loss instead of silently dropping them.
+              errors.push(
+                `Cannot preserve sub-events: the first replacement event at path [${pathForLog}] does not support sub-events.`
+              );
             }
```

Note the message names the PATH of the offending event, because the caller
needs to be able to say "this specific event" rather than "something went
wrong". That is the same convention the surrounding code already uses.


WHY NOT FAIL THE WHOLE CALL
---------------------------
Returning an error without applying anything would leave the original event
intact, which is arguably cleaner — but it also means the primary requested
edit does not happen, and the caller has no partial state to inspect. The
existing convention in this function is "apply what you can, collect what
failed into `errors`"; this fix follows it rather than inventing a second
policy. Reviewers who prefer the stricter behaviour would change the call site
rather than this branch.


TESTS
-----
Added to `ApplyEventsChanges.spec.js`:
- a target event with 2 sub-events replaced by a list whose FIRST event is a
  comment → the call reports an error naming the path, and (asserted) the
  sub-events are not silently re-homed elsewhere;
- the regression: a standard-event-first replacement still preserves the
  sub-events exactly as before.
